\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces The alignment functor \(F\colon  \symcal {C}_{\text  {acc}} \to \symcal {C}_{\text  {erg}}\) mapping the Accusative case category (source, blue panel) to the Ergative case category (target, amber panel). All eight case roles appear in both categories; purple horizontal arrows show the functor's object-level mapping \(F(\text  {role})\). The key structural difference is in the \textbf  {morphism grouping}: the Accusative category groups \(\{S, A\} \to \text  {NOM}\) (kernel \(\{(S,A)\}\), cf. \citep  {eq:eq-2-3}), while the Ergative groups \(\{S, P\} \to \text  {ABS}\) (kernel \(\{(S,P)\}\), cf. \citep  {eq:eq-2-4}). The functor preserves the role inventory but restructures the morphism pattern---formalizing the alignment-as-functor principle. This diagram provides one link in the Morita equivalence chain of \citep  {eq:eq-6-1} that enables inter-theoretic bridge transfer.}}{35}{figure.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:functor-alignment}{{16}{35}{The alignment functor \(F\colon \mathcal {C}_{\text {acc}} \to \mathcal {C}_{\text {erg}}\) mapping the Accusative case category (source, blue panel) to the Ergative case category (target, amber panel). All eight case roles appear in both categories; purple horizontal arrows show the functor's object-level mapping \(F(\text {role})\). The key structural difference is in the \textbf {morphism grouping}: the Accusative category groups \(\{S, A\} \to \text {NOM}\) (kernel \(\{(S,A)\}\), cf. \citep {eq:eq-2-3}), while the Ergative groups \(\{S, P\} \to \text {ABS}\) (kernel \(\{(S,P)\}\), cf. \citep {eq:eq-2-4}). The functor preserves the role inventory but restructures the morphism pattern---formalizing the alignment-as-functor principle. This diagram provides one link in the Morita equivalence chain of \citep {eq:eq-6-1} that enables inter-theoretic bridge transfer}{figure.16}{}}
